# Origin:  none -- Veil-authored. There is no upstream counterpart: this patch corrects
#          veil/006-font-fingerprint.patch, which is deliberately left byte-identical to the port
#          so its BSD-3 attribution stays a statement about adryfish's code and nothing else. See
#          patches/veil/README.md -> "Veil-authored patches" for the 9NN numbering rule and why a
#          deviation lives in its own file.
# Source:  n/a -- authored in this repository.
# Commit:  n/a -- see this repository's git log for this file.
# License: BSD-3-Clause (see CREDITS.md), same terms as the code it edits.
# Changes: n/a -- not a port. clearcotelabs/clearcote-browser's 040-fonts was audited as the
#          counterpart and is NOT the source of this patch: its MetricCompatibleSubstitute() opens
#          with `if (host_os != "linux") return std::string();` so it never executes on a Windows
#          host, and its deterministic-absence rule reads ungoogled::CurrentPersona().fonts, a
#          second identity source Veil rejected in 5.5b. One idea is shared, and credited as an
#          argument rather than as code: absence must follow a reason, not a probability.
#
# Justification is measurement, not review. Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.3 scored assertion 6
# over the committed corpus and found exactly one `defect` on the shipping engine: the font list of
# `151.0.7922.137-1.1001` / persona, which claims macOS and enumerates
#
#   macos:   Apple Color Emoji, Baskerville, Geneva, Hiragino Sans, Menlo, Monaco, Optima, PingFang SC
#   windows: Segoe UI, Microsoft Sans Serif, Microsoft PhagsPa
#
# at the same time. No machine runs two operating systems. 6.8 records what this patch measured.
#
# FOUR mechanisms produced that snapshot, and they are independent. Note that 006 substitutes -- it
# does not merely hide -- so the plan's "hiding alone cannot be right on a Windows host" framing was
# wrong about the cause:
#
# 1. IsBasicFont returned before the platform branch, and its list held concrete faces --
#    "Segoe UI", "Microsoft Sans Serif", "Calibri", "Helvetica Neue", "Lucida Grande" -- so those
#    were present under EVERY persona, independent of the seed and of --fingerprint-platform. Two of
#    the three leaked Windows families came from there, and the same list would have leaked
#    "Helvetica Neue" and "Lucida Grande" onto a WINDOWS persona from a host that had them, which is
#    the same contradiction pointing the other way. Split rather than deleted: a CSS generic keyword,
#    `system-ui`, `-webkit-standard`, `BlinkMacSystemFont` and the X11-era `Sans` are not families a
#    page can find absent, they are the fallback path the rest of this block depends on. They stay
#    exempt (IsGenericFontFamily). Every concrete face now answers to the claimed platform's table.
#
# 2. Absence was probabilistic -- 0.95 for a non-platform family cross-OS, 0.02 per family same-OS.
#    "Microsoft PhagsPa" is the 5%, which is not a rare event when a page asks about 85 families.
#    Replaced cross-OS by membership: the claimed platform presents a family if its canonical table
#    lists it, and does not if it does not, for every seed. Same-OS, the probability is deleted and
#    not replaced -- see "deliberately not done" below, where the measurement says why.
#
# 3. The macOS table was polluted with Windows faces. Ten of them, where 6.5 named one. Only TWO are
#    deleted, and the reason for each side is the point:
#      deleted -- "Microsoft Sans Serif": a Windows system UI face with no Apple or Office-for-Mac
#                 distribution channel, and the one name coherence.mjs's OS_ONLY_FONTS.windows uses
#                 to carry a platform claim. If Apple does ship it, the correction belongs in
#                 OS_ONLY_FONTS rather than here, and the cost of this line is one family a Mac
#                 might have and this persona says it does not -- the mild direction of error.
#      deleted -- "Carlito": a metric-compatible Calibri clone distributed with LibreOffice and by
#                 Linux distributions. Neither Apple nor Microsoft ships it to a Mac; it is a
#                 Linux-ism that reached the macOS branch by copying.
#      kept    -- "Georgia", "Verdana", "Trebuchet MS", "Comic Sans MS", "Impact", "Tahoma",
#                 "Webdings", "Wingdings": Microsoft's cross-platform web and Office faces, which
#                 arrive on a Mac with Office. Deleting them would build the opposite
#                 implausibility -- a Mac with Office but no Georgia -- and not one of the eight is
#                 in OS_ONLY_FONTS, precisely because Office is a second distribution channel.
#    The set the table now describes is a Mac carrying Microsoft's core web fonts and the classic
#    Office faces (Garamond, Rockwell, Bookman Old Style, Book Antiqua, Century Gothic) but not the
#    ClearType six (Calibri, Cambria, Candara, Consolas, Constantia, Corbel). That is one real
#    machine among several rather than a contradiction, so the six are deliberately NOT added:
#    deleting a false name is a fix, adding a name is a new claim. Recorded in 6.8 as revisitable.
#
# 4. The substitute was not required to resolve. 006 took current_os_fonts[hash % size]
#    unconditionally, and when that Windows face was not installed on the host the family came back
#    ABSENT -- which is why "Avenir" and "Didot" are missing from the -1.1001 persona while "Menlo"
#    is present. The present set was therefore a function of which random substitute happened to
#    exist: a per-family lottery in a different costume. The substitute now walks the host table
#    from the same (seed, family)-derived offset until one resolves.
#
# THREE things deliberately not done, and the second is a measurement rather than a preference:
#
# - No substitution in the same-OS branch. A family in the table but absent from the host is
#   reported absent, which is what a machine without that font reports. Substituting would trade a
#   truthful absence for a present family carrying another face's advance widths -- and it would put
#   a monospace request on a proportional face.
#
# - **No masking of the host's own set in the same-OS branch.** The first draft of this patch applied
#   the membership rule to both branches, and it was built and measured before being rejected (6.8).
#   A strict "present only if the canonical table lists it" rule also hides the family Settings names
#   for `serif`, `sans-serif` and `monospace`: on this zh-CN host those are localised names that no
#   English table contains, so all three generics fell through to the last-resort face and the probe
#   measured 1040x80 for every one of them, against 792x72 / 1124x105 / 1210x104 before. A machine
#   whose serif, sans-serif and monospace are the same face is a louder tell than the host's spare
#   Office fonts, and "Arial" then reads *absent* because the generic it is compared against became
#   Arial. Fixing it needs the Settings font families, which live in FontSelector and not in
#   FontCache -- the same dependency `972` needs in order to make generic resolution follow the
#   claimed platform, so the same-OS half of this surface lands there (T12). What this patch does
#   keep in the same-OS branch is the one contradiction it can score, and it is exactly the rule
#   assertion 6's font-set-belongs-to-one-os states: a family the claimed platform's table does NOT
#   list while another platform's does is hidden. A Windows machine that some application gave
#   "Menlo" or "Lucida Grande" therefore does not enumerate two operating systems. Both clauses
#   matter -- dropping the first one hides the thirteen families the Windows and macOS tables share
#   (Arial, Georgia, Verdana, Tahoma, ...), measured at 44/85 against 57 before it was added back.
#   The measured cost of the rule as written is one family: "Arial Narrow" is on this host and in
#   the macOS table but missing from 006's Windows table, so a Windows persona reports it absent.
#   That is a machine without Office, which exists; the fix is a completer Windows table, not a
#   looser rule.
#
# - The advance widths of a cross-OS persona are still the host's, and this patch cannot fix that.
#   Reporting Menlo with Menlo's metrics needs Menlo's outlines; Windows ships no metric-compatible
#   clone of it, and Veil bundles no font files. That residual is measured in 6.8 and registered in
#   coherence.mjs's notScorableHere as a two-snapshot check, because a single snapshot cannot tell
#   "Menlo measures like Menlo" from "Menlo measures like Tahoma" without a font-metric spec sheet.
#
# No switch is added. The behaviour is driven entirely by --fingerprint and --fingerprint-platform,
# which 000 already declares and 006 already reads, so the flag dialect does not grow and none of the
# three mirrors moves. `--disable-spoofing=font` still opts the whole block out.
#
# Compiles with the two warning classes 006 already produces, neither introduced here and neither
# fatal in this configuration: -Wexit-time-destructors on the function-local statics, and
# -Wshorten-64-to-32 on 006's own `uint32_t hash = std::hash<std::string>{}(...)` line.
#
